# Patient search

The patient lookup flow's result surface: the patient info header
that confirms *which patient* before any clinical content renders.

<%= embed :patient_search, :default %>

## When to use

- At the top of any page scoped to a single patient — the constant
  banner answering "am I looking at the right record?".
- As the landing surface after a patient lookup.

## When not to use

- Listing multiple patients — that is a `table` of results; this
  header presents exactly one confirmed patient.
- Non-patient entities (teams, clinics) — compose the same
  primitives into a purpose-built header instead of borrowing this
  one.

## Composition

A domain composite, not a primitive — it builds on:

- the avatar treatment (initials fallback) for the patient marker,
- the muted card surface (`bg-muted/50`, border, radius) from the
  card pattern,
- the *Typography* supporting-text step for identifiers (UR number,
  DOB) under the patient name.

Build new domain composites this way: assemble existing primitives
in a partial, document the data contract, never fork component
styles.

## Usage

`render_patient_info_header` (`Gesso::Components::PatientInfoHeaderHelper`)
takes a single `patient` hash and renders the identity banner; it
derives the avatar initials and the name + gender display.

```erb
<%%= render_patient_info_header(patient: {
  first_name: "Jane", last_name: "Smith", gender: "F",
  urn: "1234567", dob: "15 Mar 1985",
  address: "123 Main St, Sydney NSW 2000"
}) %>
```

The `patient` hash: `first_name`, `last_name`, `gender`, `urn`, plus the
identifier fields shown in the metadata row (`dob`, `address`).

## Do and don't

- Do render it before any clinical data on patient-scoped pages —
  wrong-patient errors are the failure mode this component exists to
  prevent.
- Do keep the identifier row to identifiers (UR, DOB) — it is for
  confirming identity, not summarising the record.
- Don't truncate identifiers; the name truncates on narrow screens,
  the UR number never does.
- Don't add actions into the header; it is a confirmation surface,
  not a toolbar.

## Accessibility and content

- The initials avatar is `aria-hidden` — the patient's name
  immediately beside it is the accessible identity.
- Identifiers are labelled ("UR: 123456"), not bare numbers.
- Name display is "First Last (G)" with gender shown only when
  known — absence of data renders as absence, not "Unknown".
